{0} fait tourner des modèles d'IA sur vo:
{0} runs AI models on your hardware or on your organisation's instance. Nothing goes to a third-party service until you connect a provider key yourself.

To begin:
- open a Chat and ask your question; the model used is shown under the input field, with its hosting country;
- put a document in Files so the agents can refer to it;
- choose the approval level in the bottom bar of Cowork or Code, next to the “+”: it decides what an agent may do without asking you.

Everything you write stays on your machine and on your instance. Closing the application erases nothing.

Beaucoup de services ne laissent entrer:
Many services only let in an app that your organization has declared with them. You create it once, in the service's console; after that, “Sign in” is enough. Each service's panel, in Settings, Connectors, always follows the same order:

- “Open …” buttons at the top: they lead to the exact console page where the app is created;
- numbered steps, in the console's order: what to click, what to choose, what to copy;
- the return address, with a “Copy” button, in the step where you paste it at the service. Paste it as it is: one character different and the service refuses;
- the scopes or permissions to tick, copyable too: no more, no less, otherwise the connection is refused as a precaution;
- what not to do (the wrong type of app, publishing or not);
- “If … shows an error”: the likely cause and the fix, for errors the service shows on its own site.

Then paste the ID and the secret into the panel's fields: each field says where to find them. The secret is kept encrypted on the instance and never leaves it. Many consoles show the secret only once: copy it straight away.

One app serves several services: Google's for Gmail, Calendar, Drive, Sheets, Slides, Docs, Forms and YouTube; Microsoft 365's for Outlook, OneDrive, SharePoint, Excel, Word and Teams, and for the Outlook mailbox. LinkedIn, on the other hand, wants two: one for the profile, and a second, new one for the company Page (the “LinkedIn (company Page)” row). LinkedIn only opens Pages to an app that has no other product, and reviews the request first: the organization, a business email address, and verification of the app by a super admin of the Page. It does not announce a timeframe.

The return address depends on the address through which you open the instance: on this computer, it starts with http://localhost or http://127.0.0.1. Some services want an https address (Slack for its server, sometimes Meta, LinkedIn or Pipedrive): then open the instance through its https address, and declare the one the panel shows.

Consoles change their labels from time to time: if a button no longer has exactly the name written, look for the equivalent.

L'écran Code confie un travail sur un do:
The Code screen hands work on a project folder to a coding agent. Choose the folder on the line above the input field, then write your request. The progress panel, on the right, shows what the agent is doing: files read and written, commands, tasks.

- The work continues when you leave the session: opening another session, a Chat or another page stops nothing. In the sidebar, a spinner marks a session that is still working; reopened, it picks up where it is. Only “Stop” stops the agent.
- By default, the engine is OpenCode, with the models of {0}: every action goes through your approval level.

Codex, with your ChatGPT account. On your own machine (desktop application, instance not opened to colleagues, administrator account), the “OpenCode” chip on the folder line also offers “Codex (your ChatGPT account)”. To connect it:
- install Codex, OpenAI's software: {0} does not install it for you, and gives the official command to copy;
- choose “Sign in with ChatGPT”: the sign-in finishes in your browser, at OpenAI. {0} sees neither your password nor any token;
- once connected, choose “Codex” in the same chip.

What to know before using it:
- your request and the project files Codex reads go to OpenAI (United States), within the limits of your subscription;
- Codex does not go through the approvals of {0}: only its sandbox limits it. At “Approve everything”, it changes the project's files and runs commands, with no network for those commands; at “Ask before changing”, it works read-only; at “Ask for everything”, it is not offered;
- Codex picks its own model;
- its sessions do not go into the sidebar list, and leaving the Code screen stops a Codex task in progress.

A colleague, a machine attached to an instance or a shared instance do not see Codex: a ChatGPT subscription only serves the person who holds it.

L'extension {0} pour VS Code met le Chat:
The {0} extension for VS Code puts your instance's Chat in the VS Code sidebar, with your models and your rules. It talks to no other service.

To install it:
- download its .vsix file, attached to every published version: {1};
- in VS Code, open the Extensions view, then the "…" menu at the top of the view, "Install from VSIX…", and choose the file. A newer version installs the same way, on top.

What it does:
- the {0} icon opens two tabs, Chat and Code. "Joindre le fichier ouvert" (attach the open file) sends the current file with the question;
- right-click on selected code: explain the selection, or improve it;
- every code block in an answer has an "Insérer" (insert) button above it: it replaces the selection, or goes in at the cursor;
- while the model is thinking, the view says for how many seconds;
- the Code tab has the code agent work on the open folder, with your approval cards ("Autoriser" or "Refuser"). You first need the "{0} : se connecter (pour {0} Code)" command: your account, your password, and your code if two-factor authentication is on. The session is kept in the VS Code secret storage.

Its settings: the instance address (by default this computer's app; elsewhere, https only), the token (empty, it is read on this computer; for a company instance, the one your administrator gives you) and the model (empty: Auto). The Code tab only works with this computer's instance. The extension only activates in a trusted workspace. Its screens are in French.

La commande helix fait parler un termina:
The helix command lets a terminal talk to your instance, like the interface does: same models, same tools, same approval gate, same log.

To set it up: Settings, Install the apps, CLI tab, "Set up", from the desktop app, on a Mac, on Windows, or on Linux with the .deb package (not with the AppImage: the tab says so). On a Mac and on Linux, the launcher goes into ~/.local/bin, without administrator rights; if that folder is missing from the PATH, a marked line is added to your shell profile. On Windows, it is a helix.cmd file in the .helix\bin folder of your account, and that folder is added to your account's PATH, without administrator rights either. Then open a new terminal (on Windows, PowerShell or Command Prompt). "Update" and "Remove" are in the same place. Node 20 or later is needed: if it is missing, the button installs the app's own.

The main commands:
- helix connexion: sign in once with your account (hidden password, then a code if two-factor authentication is on);
- helix, or helix chat: an interactive Chat; helix chat "question": one question, one answer;
- helix chat --outils: the same Chat, with the instance's tools and connectors;
- helix code: the code agent on the current folder; helix code "request" for a single request;
- helix modeles and helix outils: the instance's models, and its tool groups with the approval level;
- helix deconnexion; helix --aide: every command and option (--adresse, --modele, --effort, --dossier…).

In a conversation: /nouveau, /modele, /aide, /quitter (or Ctrl+D); Ctrl+C stops the current answer.

When the agent wants to act, the terminal shows the whole request and asks "Autoriser ? [o/N]": only "o" or "oui" grants it, any other answer refuses. Without an answer within two minutes, the action is not taken. Away from this computer, the instance address must be https.

Le sélecteur de modèles du Chat propose:
The Chat model picker offers a short list suited to this computer. To choose among all the open models (Apache 2.0 or MIT licences): model picker, “Install a model on this computer”, then “See all models”.

The “Models” page:
- search by name, publisher or country;
- sort by score (Epoch AI) or by size;
- filters: publisher, reads images, reasons, fast without a graphics card;
- for each model: publisher and country, licence, download size, score or “no published score”, capabilities.

Only the models this computer runs without slowing down can be installed. Heavier ones stay visible, greyed out, with the reason: the memory they need, and the memory this computer has. A lighter model answers faster, with simpler answers.

One installation at a time: the download is shown on the page, then the model appears in the picker. The model set up at first launch stays the one recommended for this computer; the setup screen also offers the others, through “Choose another model”.

Par défaut, rien ne sort de votre instal:
By default, nothing leaves your installation. What can leave, and only if you connected it yourself:

- a model at a provider with your key: the conversation goes to them;
- a connector (mailbox, storage, messaging): it talks to the service you designated;
- the update check, to the address written into the package by your provider, or to your organisation's instance. An update only installs if it carries the signature of your application's publisher, wherever it comes from.

You can export all your data at any time, or ask for its erasure, from Settings, Privacy. The audit log keeps a trace of every action taken by an agent.

Quand une demande est trop grosse pour u:
When a request is too large for a single turn, the model is called a first time to judge whether it should be split, then to write the plan. Each step is then handled separately, with its own budget of actions, and checked before moving to the next.

The splitting is not infinite, and that is deliberate:
- 15 steps at the first level, 8 when a step is split again;
- 3 levels of depth;
- 60 steps executed in total for one request.

A request that reaches these limits stops and says so, rather than running for ever. The plan is shown as it goes, with the steps done and those remaining.

Réglages, Abonnement présente l'offre d':
Settings, Subscription presents {0}'s model hosting offer. The software is free: what would be paid for is the computing of the hosted models.

The page shows:
- open-weight models, hosted in Europe: all-round and fast. Each plan gives a monthly computing credit, which each model uses up at its own rate, on what it reads and on what it writes;
- the plans, monthly or yearly: for individuals (Discovery, Plus, Pro, Max) and for businesses (Team and Team Premium, per seat; Enterprise, on quote);
- for each, the tokens per model and their approximate equivalent in Chat exchanges per day or tasks per month, and, monthly, the launch price for the first months;
- what each plan includes: the whole platform, the machine's own models included.

What the page does not do: these plans are not open. No payment is possible from this screen, and the prices are a proposal, which may change. "Write to be notified" and "Ask for a quote" open an email in your mail app, addressed to support: nothing goes out without you.

Without a subscription, nothing changes: your machine's models, and those you connect with your own key, remain available to you.

Une compétence est une manière de faire,:
A skill is a way of doing things, written in plain words, which {0} applies when the situation calls for it. For instance: how a meeting report is written where you work, what a quote must state, in what order to invoice.

It is created in Cowork, right-hand panel, “Procedures” card, “Create a skill” button. Three fields, and not one more:

- the name, to find it again;
- when to use it: this is the only sentence the model reads to decide whether to apply it, so describe the situation, not the method;
- the procedure: the steps in order, and what must never be done. Write it as you would for a new colleague.

What you should know:

- a skill gives no new power: it says how to use the ones that already exist. To connect a new service, that is Connectors;
- it applies in Cowork, in Chat and to tasks carried out by an agent;
- the switch sets it aside without erasing it;
- “The whole organisation” makes it readable by your colleagues, who cannot modify it;
- the instructions are sent again with every message: the line under the list says how many characters go out. Beyond a dozen procedures, the last ones no longer go, and the screen says so rather than cutting them in two.
